# MAINTAINERS
#
# Who reviews what.  The counterpart of `AUTHORS`: that file lists everyone who
# has contributed, in the order they did; this one says who is responsible for
# each part of the tree **now**, and it changes when responsibility does.
#
# What each tag means, and the order an entry lists them in:
#
#   M:  the person who reviews a change to the listed paths
#   R:  someone who reviews but does not have the final say
#   L:  a list a change is sent to (none yet: the tree has no list)
#   F:  a file or directory pattern this entry covers
#   T:  a summary, when the entry is about a subject rather than a path
#
# One entry ends at the next blank line.  A path may appear in more than one
# entry; the most specific match is the reviewer for a change, which is the
# rule `scripts/` will implement when there is a script — until then this file
# is read by people, and a person resolves the same way.
#
# A change touching several subsystems is sent to each entry's maintainer.  A
# maintainer who is also the author of a change is not its reviewer, which is
# why the whole tree has a fallback entry below the subsystems.
#
# Adding an entry: name the paths, name the person, and do not invent a role
# you have not agreed to.  An entry that names nobody is worse than no entry:
# it reads as coverage and answers nothing.

